insight('polynomial-division','Division, quotient/remainder and scope boundaries',[P(3,1),P(3,2),P(3,3)],
 'Goldie begins with aligned arithmetic, then cubic/linear division and several equivalent worked routes;its1A set moves from linear to quadratic divisors. YM separates linear and quadratic division and explicitly includes zero columns, nonmonic divisors and other variable letters. YMExample15 only asks a remainder although its solution works a quotient;Example16 asks both. GoldiePrompt3 asks why a reduced remainder has lower degree. These are different required outputs, not interchangeable answer fields.',
 'NSSCAS2023P2Q1(a) and2024P2Q5(a) give observed division structures. The latter source p(x) defect remains registered; its existence does not undermine exact division of the intended polynomial. Official awards, not the quantity of textbook working, decide later method credit. The three-brackets-without-working restriction in2023Q1(b) belongs to the connected factorisation part, not every division task.',
 'GradeA11 explicitly permits degree-at-most-four dividends and linear/quadratic divisors. It does not impose that dividend limit on unrelated multiplication or theorem evaluation. Nonmonic scaling, sparse columns and linear versus scalar remainder are meaningful difficulty branches;larger coefficients alone are not. No numeric grade parameter domain is set here.',
 'YMExample14 changes its dividend constant in a later displayed identity;Try15 omits requested quotients in the key. These source defects are recorded. Independent reconstruction P=DQ+R and degree checks can catch both arithmetic and wrong-output mistakes. The textbook cautions do not establish an examiner-reported rate of those errors.',
 'Both books provide substantial forward division practice;Goldie has explicit conceptual remainder-degree support. A remainder-only theorem exercise does not cover asked quotient recovery, and a surrounding quadratic-division heading does not makeYMTry16Q2(c) a quadratic-divisor question. Supporting high-power arithmetic must not create out-of-scope division coverage.',
 'Propose distinct output contracts for quotient only,remainder only and both, with an invariant reconstruction check. Preserve linear/nonmonic and quadratic remainder branches. Treat supplied quotient identities separately from freely chosen algorithms; derive future diagram/long-division layouts from the original coefficients.',
 [E('Goldie',1,'Example:4'),E('Goldie',1,'Example:13'),E('Goldie',1,'Prompt:3'),E('Goldie',1,'Exercise-1A:10'),E('YM',8,'Example:14'),E('YM',8,'Example:15'),E('YM',8,'Example:16'),E('YM',8,'Try-Now:15'),E('YM',8,'Try-Now-16:2')],
 ['8227:EXAM:2023:P2:Q1:a','8227:EXAM:2024:P2:Q5:a'],['8227:GRADE:A:11'],['8227:CONVENTION:037'])
insight('remainder-and-factor-constraints','Theorem evaluation, inverse constraints and factorisation',[P(4,1),P(4,2),P(4,3),P(4,4)],
 'Goldie moves from value tables and integer-candidate tests to factor/solve/sketch chains, then inverse coefficient constraints. YM explicitly contrasts quotient/remainder computation with evaluation without division, then factor proofs, complete factorisation and polynomial solves. Both use nonmonic divisor roots exactly. YM19Q1 adds an unknown exponent;20Q2 uses an unspecifiedR and2R;Goldie1B14/YM23Q3 yield multiple parameter tuples. These are authored structural branches of one constraint-building decision, not a recipe per set of numbers.',
 'NSSCAS2021P2Q1(a)(b),2025P2Q1 and2023P2Q1(b)(c) anchor inverse constraints and factor-to-root chains. Convention037 explicitly denies2023Q1(b)marks for three correct brackets without working; do not infer that all factorisations everywhere have that exact award. Textbooks additionally ask full sketches, exponent recovery or transformed-variable roots that the cited parts do not.',
 'GradeE10 names single-unknown linear theorem work;C10 broadens coefficient evaluation and polynomial solving;A11 names combined factor/remainder constraints. Nonlinear parameter tuples, parameter-dependent divisor roots and original-variable recovery increase reasoning demands. The title of a harder exercise is not evidence of a higher assessed Bloom level or a grade assigned here.',
 'GoldieExample9 demonstrates that no integer root is not no real root. YMExercise8 needs both distinct roots to prove a quadratic factor. Try27 has a wrong |x| recovery;Exercise18 needs secant/cosine range rejection. ERR10 supports original-variable recovery broadly. Wrong printed factors or copied signs are source defects, not demonstrated learner misconceptions.',
 'Both books provide inverse constraints and factor/solve chains;Goldie adds a richer factor-to-sketch progression, whileYM supplies exponent,composition and trigonometric recovery. Root multiplicity, irreducible real factors and complete original-domain checking need to remain visible. No absence from these textbook families changes the138-demand syllabus map.',
 'Propose retaining a constraint ledger that distinguishes zero,known,equal and scaled remainders. Verify every recovered tuple in every original condition. Keep inverse recovery and sketch extensions connected where asked. A candidate-root search must record its admitted set and scope of any failure conclusion.',
 [E('Goldie',1,'Example:9'),E('Goldie',1,'Exercise-1B:5'),E('Goldie',1,'Exercise-1B:14'),E('YM',8,'Try-Now-19:1'),E('YM',8,'Try-Now-20:2'),E('YM',8,'Try-Now-23:3'),E('YM',8,'Exercise-8:8'),E('YM',8,'Try-Now:27'),E('YM',8,'Exercise-8:18')],
 ['8227:EXAM:2021:P2:Q1:a','8227:EXAM:2021:P2:Q1:b','8227:EXAM:2025:P2:Q1:answer','8227:EXAM:2023:P2:Q1:b','8227:EXAM:2023:P2:Q1:c'],['8227:GRADE:E:10','8227:GRADE:C:10','8227:GRADE:A:11'],['8227:CONVENTION:037'],['8227:ERR10'])
insight('modulus-sign-and-sets','Absolute-value meaning, equations and complete inequalities',[P(1,1),P(1,2),P(1,3)],
 'YM introduces nonnegative physical error magnitudes before grouping/nested bars,then isolates the modulus and distinguishes positive,zero and negative targets. Goldie starts with a function-value prompt and develops graphical distance/inside-outside reasoning. Both compare two moduli;YM7Q1 asks two methods whereasGoldieExample18 reuses equality crossings in an inequality. GoldieExample16 reverses a supplied interval into a centre/radius modulus statement, a direction less prominent inYM.',
 'NSSCAS2021P2Q3,2022P2Q1(a)(b),2024P2Q11(b)(c) and2025P2Q2 provide observed modulus solving/connected structures. Textbook number lines,reverse interval encoding,physical deviations and the prescribed two-method instruction are extra evidence, not assumed official marks. A correct intermediate root list does not answer a transformed output or bounded-angle part.',
 'E10 includes modulus notation/equations;C10 andA11 support equations and inequalities with broader combinations. The main increase is sign logic:constant target versus variable RHS,two nonnegative sides versus a line,empty/all-real cases and coupled original-variable recovery. A fixed positive symbolic parameter may change the midpoint without adding numerical difficulty. Grade boundaries are qualitative here.',
 'GoldieExample17 explicitly requires care before squaring a comparison with a potentially negative side;YMExample6 filters a negative-RHS candidate. YMTry12(f)key misses an all-real solution,Goldie1C7 reverses its half-line,andYMExample12 has faulty equality-check prose. These registered source defects justify independent original-relation tests. No matching specific modulus learner-error frequency is claimed from the examiner register.',
 'Both books offer routine and two-modulus branches;Goldie provides inverse interval representation,YM negative/zero targets and explicit number lines. YMExercise10 asks coordinate pairs,Try26Q2 asks new output values,andRevision8Q4 asks bounded cotangent recovery. Their shared topic does not make their response evidence equivalent.',
 'Propose sign-aware branches and intentional empty/singleton/all-real cases rather than treating them as invalid instances. Keep original-domain checks and interval endpoint logic inspectable. Future feedback should distinguish incomplete branching,extraneous roots and correct roots used to answer the wrong requested output.',
 [E('YM',8,'Example:1'),E('YM',8,'Example:5'),E('YM',8,'Example:6'),E('YM',8,'Try-Now-7:1'),E('YM',8,'Try-Now:12'),E('YM',8,'Exercise-8:10'),E('YM',8,'Try-Now-26:2'),E('YM',8,'Revision-Exercise-8:4'),E('Goldie',1,'Example:16'),E('Goldie',1,'Example:17'),E('Goldie',1,'Exercise-1C:7')],
 ['8227:EXAM:2021:P2:Q3:answer','8227:EXAM:2022:P2:Q1:a','8227:EXAM:2022:P2:Q1:b','8227:EXAM:2024:P2:Q11:b','8227:EXAM:2024:P2:Q11:c','8227:EXAM:2025:P2:Q2:answer'],['8227:GRADE:E:10','8227:GRADE:C:10','8227:GRADE:A:11'])
insight('modulus-graphs-and-input-direction','Modulus sketches, interpretation and missing reverse-graph practice',[P(2,1),P(2,2)],
 'GoldieExample14 constructs a line,reflects its negative branch and translates the resulting V;Exercise1C4 gives separate transformation sketches. YM builds from|x|and transformations,thenExample9 separates intercepts,vertex and full sketch. It later couples two parameterised graphs and an inequality inExample26. In both books most retained tasks give the equation and ask a graph;source solution pictures must not be recast as learner givens.',
 'NSSCAS2023P2Q2 reads supplied graph data to recover unknown information;2024P2Q11(a) asks sketches then later deduction. The raw inventory tagsYMExample9 and26 with interpretation,but both interpret functions the learner is constructing. That tag alone does not cover the exam given-graph→unknown-parameter direction. The contrast is a real practice gap,not a reason to manufacture a new textbook item.',
 'C10 supports basic modulus sketches;A11 includes combined scaled/shifted form. QUAL01 still limits original graph coefficients to integers. YMTry9Q1(c) has fractional vertex5/2but integer original coefficients,whereasTry9Q2(e) has original coefficient-1/3and is outside. Example26is valid algebra for allpositivea,but its graph branch fitsQUAL01only for positive integers. No numerical grade domains are settled here.',
 'YMExample8 has correct shifted graph but wrong prose sign for its horizontal shift. Fractional vertices must not be confused with fractional coefficients;allintercepts/slopes must agree with the original formula. These are source/author cautions;no examiner-confirmed modulus-specific misconception count is asserted.',
 'Both books strongly support equation-to-sketch. The given-graph reconstruction task is thin in the inspected books despite exam evidence. YMRevision8Q3 broadens to a two-graph root-count demonstration involving a quartic;Exercise19 overlays a parabola and|x|after a negative-root parameter condition. These are visual reasoning branches beyond oneV-sketch.',
 'Propose explicit input-direction metadata:given formula→draw graph versusgiven graph→infer parameters. Retain shared axes and dependencies in coupled tasks. Allfuture graph assets must derive labels/vertices/intersections from common instance values. Keep fractional-coefficient source cases excluded from8227designwhile retaining them as internal source evidence.',
 [E('Goldie',1,'Example:14'),E('Goldie',1,'Exercise-1C:4'),E('YM',8,'Example:8'),E('YM',8,'Example:9'),E('YM',8,'Try-Now-9:1'),E('YM',8,'Try-Now-9:2'),E('YM',8,'Example:26'),E('YM',8,'Revision-Exercise-8:3'),E('YM',8,'Exercise-8:19')],
 ['8227:EXAM:2023:P2:Q2:answer','8227:EXAM:2024:P2:Q11:a','8227:EXAM:2024:P2:Q11:c'],['8227:GRADE:C:10','8227:GRADE:A:11'])

